  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates an upper level ridge centered across \nthe Western High Plains. Near the surface, a weak lee side \ntrough of low pressure remains anchored in eastern Colorado.\n\nAlthough relatively dry conditions will dominate across much\nof the area early in the period, minimal thunderstorm chances \n(<20%) cannot be ruled out near the Colorado line early in the \nperiod as the SREF indicates the aforementioned ridge axis \nshifting slowly east into the Central Plains, giving way to a \nsouthwesterly flow aloft across the Western High Plains. Near \nthe surface, prevailing south- southeasterlies will support \npooling moisture ahead of a lee side trough axis slowly \napproaching the Colorado/Kansas line, resulting in modest MUCAPE\nvalues in excess of 500 to 1000 J/kg. As a result, diurnally \ndriven thunderstorm development is forecast across eastern \nColorado in vicinity of the trough axis late this afternoon in \nconjunction with H5 vort maxima ejecting out of the Southern \nRockies. Storms are expected to drift east-northeast toward the \nKansas line during the evening. This is hinted at by the latest \nHREF pointing to only 10 to 20% probability of 6-hr QPF \nexceeding 0.1 of an inch in west central/extreme southwest \nKansas by late evening. A similar set up exists Tuesday, though \nstorm chances (30-50%) pick up a bit out west as an upper level \nshortwave trough transitions east through the Dakotas, sending \nan attendant cold front southeast into extreme western Kansas \novernight.\n\nUnseasonably hot temperatures are likely again today as\nprevailing southerlies reinforce a much warmer air mass across \nthe area with H85 temperatures ranging from the upper 20s(C) in \ncentral Kansas to around 30C near the Colorado line. Look for \nwidespread afternoon highs in the mid/upper 90s(F) with the HREF\npainting a better than 80% probability of temperatures \nexceeding 95F for much of the area with highs potentially \ntopping 100F in central/south central Kansas where there is a \n50-70% probability of reaching the century mark. Similar \ntemperatures are forecast Tuesday.",
